{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01X7CS8heEe5B5Lbs8rf2mYf","type":"tool_result","content":"855\timpl Quic {\n856\t    /// Read the virtual time tls sees through its `get_time`\n857\t    /// callback (microseconds).  C: `get_tls_time`.\n858\t    ///\n859\t    /// The C body asks the TLS context for milliseconds and converts\n860\t    /// them back to microseconds.  Rust keeps QUIC time in the context\n861\t    /// helper, so this returns the same microsecond value exposed by\n862\t    /// [`Quic::time`].\n863\t    pub fn tls_time(&self) -> u64 {\n864\t        self.time()\n865\t    }\n866\t}\n867\t\n868\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n869\t// Random number generation.\n870\t//\n871\t// The crypto-grade RNG is the tls / OpenSSL provider; the public RNG\n872\t// is xorshift1024* seeded from the crypto RNG.  Both take a\n873\t// destination slice; the C `(void* buf, size_t len)` pair collapses\n874\t// to `&mut [u8]`.\n875\t\n876\t// `crypto_random` / `crypto_uniform_random` / `seed_public_random`\n877\t// are gone -- callers reach `Quic.rng` (a `Box<dyn CryptoRng>`)\n878\t// directly and use the `rand::Rng` / `rand::RngCore` methods on\n879\t// it.  The public-RNG helpers (`public_random_64` etc.) likewise\n880\t// disappear: callers use `rand::rng()` for the thread-local\n881\t// non-secure stream.\n882\t\n883\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n884\t// AEAD primitives."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c83abba6-1d49-4ee8-837e-74d6c23d507c","uuid":"6726c6a8-1896-4b0c-a818-4a762257a331","timestamp":"2026-05-07T09:48:28.260Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/richbarn/Projects/picoquic/rs/fq/src/tls_api.rs","content":"impl Quic {\n    /// Read the virtual time tls sees through its `get_time`\n    /// callback (microseconds).  C: `get_tls_time`.\n    ///\n    /// The C body asks the TLS context for milliseconds and converts\n    /// them back to microseconds.  Rust keeps QUIC time in the context\n    /// helper, so this returns the same microsecond value exposed by\n    /// [`Quic::time`].\n    pub fn tls_time(&self) -> u64 {\n        self.time()\n    }\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Random number generation.\n//\n// The crypto-grade RNG is the tls / OpenSSL provider; the public RNG\n// is xorshift1024* seeded from the crypto RNG.  Both take a\n// destination slice; the C `(void* buf, size_t len)` pair collapses\n// to `&mut [u8]`.\n\n// `crypto_random` / `crypto_uniform_random` / `seed_public_random`\n// are gone -- callers reach `Quic.rng` (a `Box<dyn CryptoRng>`)\n// directly and use the `rand::Rng` / `rand::RngCore` methods on\n// it.  The public-RNG helpers (`public_random_64` etc.) likewise\n// disappear: callers use `rand::rng()` for the thread-local\n// non-secure stream.\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// AEAD primitives.","numLines":30,"startLine":855,"totalLines":1891}}}
